WEBVTT

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:31.720
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. A couple of years ago, one of our producers put together this story that just got too big for our show. It couldn't be contained in an hour or two hours. And so it spun out into its own podcast series called The Retrievals. It was a big hit. Maybe you heard it. Our coworkers at Serial at The New York Times produced it.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。几年前，我们的一位制作人整理了这个故事，由于内容太丰富，以至于我们的节目无法容纳。它无法被限制在一两个小时内。因此，它衍生出了自己的播客系列，名为《取回者》（The Retrievals）。它非常受欢迎。也许你听过。我们的同事，《纽约时报》的Serial制作了这个节目。

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:53.920
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, now that producer, Susan Burton, is back with a story that is just as compelling. And they have released it as season two of The Retrievals. At first, I heard drafts of this months ago. And I am very excited that we can present one of the episodes for you today. I have to say, one of the things that I really like about this story is that by the end, it turns out to be surprisingly hopeful.
<v Ira Glass>总之，现在那位制作人苏珊·伯顿带着一个同样引人入胜的故事回归了。他们将其作为《取回者》的第二季发布。起初，我在几个月前就听过草稿。我非常激动今天能为大家呈现其中一集。我必须说，我非常喜欢这个故事的一点是，到最后，它竟然出奇地充满希望。

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:11.020
<v Ira Glass>She also invented this way to tell a radio story I have never heard anybody try. I hope you like it. I think you'll like it. The story takes place in a hospital's labor and delivery unit. And there's some description in here of medical procedures. And things don't go well. Just a heads-up. Here's Susan.
<v Ira Glass>她还发明了一种我从未听说过有人尝试过的广播故事讲述方式。我希望你喜欢它。我想你会喜欢的。这个故事发生在一所医院的劳产科病房。其中包含一些对医疗程序的描述。而且事情进展得并不顺利。先预警一下。这是苏珊。

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:34.010
<v Susan Burton>6:00 AM in Chicago. Of course, the shot starts at the lake. Camera pans over the water and the magnificent buildings rise from the shoreline, twinkling in the dawn light. We continue across the city, still bird's-eye view, over the grid, over the elevated tracks and wide highways, coming to rest in a western suburb.
<v Susan Burton>芝加哥时间上午6点。当然，镜头从湖边开始。摄像机掠过水面，壮丽的建筑从岸边升起，在黎明的光线下闪烁。我们继续穿过城市，依然是鸟瞰视角，掠过街道网格，掠过高架铁轨和宽阔的高速公路，最终停在西郊。

00:01:34.010 --> 00:02:04.380
<v Susan Burton>Drop down to a brick bungalow where, inside, a woman, late 30s, moves softly past the bedrooms where her children sleep and steps out the front door to meet the morning. Wind chill of 20 below. She hurries to a minivan. And as she pulls away from the curb, we see the streets frosted with salt, the moon shining high and hard. And we see her work ID where she keeps it on the center console. The words "BOARD CERTIFIED RN" and her name in all caps.
<v Susan Burton>镜头下降到一栋红砖平房，屋内，一位30多岁的女性轻轻走过孩子们熟睡的卧室，走出前门迎接清晨。风寒指数零下20度。她匆忙走向一辆小型货车。当她从路边驶离时，我们看到街道上结着霜，月亮又高又亮。我们看到她放在中央控制台上的工作证。上面写着“委员会认证注册护士”，以及她的大写姓名。

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.540
<v Susan Burton>This is Mindy Figueroa. Mindy says she was made to be a nurse.
<v Susan Burton>这就是明迪·菲格罗亚。明迪说她天生就是做护士的料。

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:30.780
<v Mindy Figueroa>I think I was just born to be in healthcare. Like, as a kid, my sister would find all the dolls and the ballet slippers at the Dollar Tree, like, section. But I always got the doctor kit, the stethoscope, the little syringe. Always, always, always. So it was just always a thing.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我觉得我生来就是做医疗行业的。就像小时候，我姐姐会在一元店找到所有的玩偶和芭蕾舞鞋，但我总是拿医生套装、听诊器、小注射器。总是，总是，总是。所以这对我来说一直是一件很自然的事。

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:53.060
<v Susan Burton>Mindy was the first one in her family to go to college. Her parents came to this country undocumented, from Mexico, worked factory jobs, earned citizenship. Mindy paid her way through school, got her degree, started out at a small hospital, and after a few years, transferred up to a big one-- UI Health at the University of Illinois at Chicago.
<v Susan Burton>明迪是她家里第一个上大学的人。她的父母从墨西哥非法移民到这个国家，做着工厂的工作，后来获得了公民身份。明迪靠自己打工付学费读完了书，拿到了学位，起初在一家小医院工作，几年后调到了一家大医院——伊利诺伊大学芝加哥分校（UIC）的UIC Health。

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.990
<v Susan Burton>She works on the labor and delivery floor, with patients about to have babies.
<v Susan Burton>她在劳产科工作，照顾即将分娩的病人。

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:14.750
<v Mindy Figueroa>I've been told many, many times that as soon as I walk in the room, they know I like my job. And I love coming to work. And I feel like they feel that. So I walk in a room and good morning, my name is Mindy. I will be here until 7:00 PM.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我被告知过很多很多次，我一走进房间，她们就知道我喜欢我的工作。而且我热爱上班。我觉得她们能感受到这一点。所以我走进房间就会说，早上好，我叫明迪。我会一直在这儿待到晚上7点。

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:41.430
<v Susan Burton>We watch this happen, Mindy stepping cheerfully into a labor room, her face framed by two neat braids. And then we carousel through shots of Mindy at work-- Mindy strapping a fetal monitor to a patient's belly, Mindy at the nursing station holding a plastic pillow up like show and tell, her colleagues doubled over in laughter, Mindy, her face grave, bursting through the door of the OR.
<v Susan Burton>我们看着这一切发生，明迪愉快地走进待产室，她的脸被两根整齐的辫子衬托着。然后我们闪回到明迪工作的镜头——明迪给病人的腹部绑上胎儿监护仪，明迪在护士站举起一个塑料枕头展示，同事们笑得直不起腰，明迪表情严肃地冲进手术室。

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:49.270
<v Susan Burton>Mindy loves the intimacy of labor and delivery. And that adrenaline, that switch from, it's calm to now it's time to run.
<v Susan Burton>明迪热爱分娩过程的亲密感。还有那种肾上腺素，那种从平静到必须立刻行动的切换。

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.150
<v Mindy Figueroa>And also, I mean, I just like blood, in general. [LAUGHS]
<v Mindy Figueroa>而且，我也很喜欢血，总的来说。[笑]

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:52.930
<v Susan Burton>You do?
<v Susan Burton>真的吗？

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.030
<v Mindy Figueroa>Yes.
<v Mindy Figueroa>是的。

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.980
<v Susan Burton>Do you mean literally, you like blood?
<v Susan Burton>你是说真的喜欢血吗？

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:23.120
<v Mindy Figueroa>Yes. And everyone on the unit knows it. If there is a hemorrhage, if there is heavy bleeding, they know I am the person. I like to weigh it. I like to quantify the blood. I like to clean it up. Blood clots coming out of after delivery and we're pushing on their belly and it's almost like popping a pimple. Like, OK, let's get it out of there to prevent more bleeding.
<v Mindy Figueroa>是的。科室里的每个人都知道这一点。如果有大出血，如果有严重的流血，他们知道我就是那个人。我喜欢称量它。我喜欢量化出血量。我喜欢清理它。分娩后血块出来，我们按压她们的肚子，感觉就像挤粉刺一样。就像，好吧，我们把它排出来以防止进一步出血。

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:57.380
<v Mindy Figueroa>And I feel like I'm really good at recognizing when we're trending towards too much blood. Because I am actually watching. I watch every birth. I watch every C-section. Like, I don't sit and chart. As soon as the incision starts, I watch it. Because it's just intriguing to me. I've been doing this for 14 years. And it still amazes me that there's an incision on an abdomen, but there's a baby there. So I watch every delivery.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我觉得我非常擅长识别我们是否正趋向于出血过多。因为我确实在看。我看每一次出生。我看每一次剖腹产。我不只是坐着写病历。切口一开始，我就盯着看。因为它对我来说太引人入胜了。我已经做了14年了。腹部开了一个口，里面却有一个婴儿，这依然让我感到惊奇。所以我看每一次分娩。

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:17.740
<v Susan Burton>This story is going to be about one of those deliveries that Mindy watched, one that changed things for her and for her hospital. There are more than 2,000 deliveries a year at UIC. It's a public hospital, not a fancy one. And it's located right in the middle of the city, in the medical district.
<v Susan Burton>这个故事将讲述明迪目睹的其中一次分娩，那次经历改变了她和她的医院。UIC每年有超过2000次分娩。这是一家公立医院，不是什么高级医院。它位于城市中心，在医疗区。

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:48.940
<v Susan Burton>Establishing shot of the hulking building, dawn breaking open the sky above. A humble hospital in Chicago, a hard-working nurse, a delivery with stakes-- how could we not tell this like a medical drama? A medical drama, its emphasis is on the world inside the hospital, on the heart and the heroism of the people who work there, on their relationships with one another, on how their interactions with patients are shaped by their own stories.
<v Susan Burton>全景镜头对准那座笨重的建筑，黎明照亮了天空。芝加哥的一家简朴医院，一位勤奋的护士，一场关系重大的分娩——我们怎么能不把它拍成一部医疗剧呢？一部医疗剧，重点在于医院内部的世界，在于在那里工作的人们的心灵和英雄主义，在于他们彼此的关系，在于他们与病人的互动如何受他们自身故事的影响。

00:05:48.940 --> 00:06:05.990
<v Susan Burton>But what happens in a hospital also reverberates outside. And on this show, we're going to see that too. Because this delivery that Mindy watches, it doesn't just change things at UIC. It could change things for the entire country.
<v Susan Burton>但医院里发生的事情也会在外部产生影响。在这个节目中，我们也会看到这一点。因为明迪目睹的这场分娩，不仅改变了UIC的事情。它可能会改变整个国家。

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:30.670
<v Susan Burton>And now let's pull back, get the whole hospital in the frame, and end the opening credit sequence with this image, this boxy building on the subzero morning. But we know that behind that imposing facade, it's warm. We're already invested in the human drama inside. Black screen.
<v Susan Burton>现在让我们拉远镜头，把整个医院纳入画面，用这个在零度清晨的方块建筑影像结束片头序列。但我们知道，在那宏伟的外表下，里面很温暖。我们已经被内部的人性故事所吸引。黑屏。

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:36.250
<v Ira Glass>Susan Burton's story continues. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·伯顿的故事继续。请留步。

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.870
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Susan Burton picks up where she left off.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。苏珊·伯顿从上次结束的地方继续。

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:58.240
<v Susan Burton>The action of the episode begins with Mindy in a nearly empty elevator. Closer to 7:00 AM, change of shift, you can barely fit, it's sardines. But Mindy's early today. She's almost always early. She's the one who will have cleaned the break room by the time her coworkers arrive.
<v Susan Burton>这一集的情节开始于一个几乎空无一人的电梯里的明迪。快到早上7点，换班时，电梯里挤得像沙丁鱼。但明迪今天来早了。她几乎总是很早。她是那种在同事到达之前就把休息室打扫干净的人。

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:19.720
<v Susan Burton>At UIC, your coworkers have your back. Mindy could say this about all of the nurses on her crew. One of those nurses is Clara Hochhauser. We see her emerging from the locker room in gray scrubs and a scrub coat. Not everyone wears a scrub coat, but Clara does because it has better pockets and because it covers her tattoos.
<v Susan Burton>在UIC，同事就是你的后盾。明迪对她组里的所有护士都可以这么说。其中一位护士是克拉拉·霍克豪瑟。我们看到她穿着灰色刷手服和刷手外套从更衣室出来。并不是每个人都穿刷手外套，但克拉拉穿是因为它有更好的口袋，而且能遮住她的纹身。

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:28.640
<v Susan Burton>Clara has large, wise eyes, as if she sees more than other people. She's a natural at nursing, but it wasn't obvious that it was where she would wind up.
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉有着大而睿智的眼睛，仿佛她能看到比别人更多的东西。她天生就是做护士的料，但这并不是她最终会从事的行业，起初并不明显。

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:43.520
<v Clara Hochhauser>I am, like, a high school dropout. I really did not do well with school. And long story short, I just was like, never mind, I'll get my GED. And I started working. And I worked in a coffee shop.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我，算是高中辍学。我在学校表现真的很不好。长话短说，我当时就想，算了吧，我拿个同等学力证书就行。于是我开始工作。我在一家咖啡店工作。

00:07:43.520 --> 00:08:04.250
<v Susan Burton>One day, Clara got a massage, and then she became a massage therapist. And she found the clients she liked best were the pregnant clients. So then she decided to become a midwife. But after finishing her degree, she just wanted to start working. And as a nurse, she could do that sooner. She started as a mother-baby nurse and eventually switched to labor and delivery.
<v Susan Burton>有一天，克拉拉做了按摩，后来她成了按摩治疗师。她发现她最喜欢的客户是孕妇。于是她决定成为一名助产士。但在完成学位后，她只想开始工作。作为一名护士，她可以更快地做到这一点。她起初是一名母婴护士，后来转到了劳产科。

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.370
<v Susan Burton>She never thought she would have her own kids.
<v Susan Burton>她从没想过自己会有孩子。

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:15.890
<v Clara Hochhauser>I definitely never planned on having children. I definitely always felt like, ooh, emotionally, a little dysfunctional family.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我绝对从来没打算要孩子。我绝对一直觉得，噢，在情感上，我家有点功能失调。

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:16.350
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:30.450
<v Clara Hochhauser>I identified, like, purely as a lesbian. I was not able to conceptualize what it meant to be bisexual. That wasn't an option. It's either you were gay or you weren't gay, you know?
<v Clara Hochhauser>我纯粹认同自己是女同性恋。我无法概念化双性恋意味着什么。那不是一个选项。你要么是同性恋，要么不是，你知道吗？

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.370
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:41.010
<v Clara Hochhauser>And not that you can't have babies with women. But just all the things, I just thought, no, no, no, no, I love little babies, but I do not want to be a parent.
<v Clara Hochhauser>并不是说你不能和女性生孩子。但种种原因，我当时就想，不不不，我喜欢小宝宝，但我不想成为家长。

00:08:41.010 --> 00:09:00.100
<v Susan Burton>And then Clara got divorced from her wife. She met a guy. They got married. Suddenly, Clara understood she didn't have to be, as she puts it, a perfect, magical, special person to have children. She was 40. With her husband, Clay, she did IVF, and soon she was pregnant with twins.
<v Susan Burton>后来克拉拉和她的妻子离婚了。她遇到了一个男人。他们结婚了。突然，克拉拉明白了她不必像她说的那样，是一个完美、神奇、特别的人才能生孩子。她40岁了。她和丈夫克莱做了试管婴儿（IVF），不久后她就怀上了双胞胎。

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:07.770
<v Clara Hochhauser>I was incredibly excited and could not believe that two embryos took when-- you know?
<v Clara Hochhauser>我非常兴奋，简直不敢相信两个胚胎都着床了，你知道吗？

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:08.180
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:30.220
<v Clara Hochhauser>It just all seemed surreal. Like, I can't believe it worked the very first transfer. But I, of course, was really nervous about the outcome because I see firsthand all the time the ways in which pregnancies can go sideways. And there are no guarantees that anybody goes home with a baby at the end. So it was just like another layer of worry.
<v Clara Hochhauser>这一切看起来太超现实了。就像，我不敢相信第一次移植就成功了。但我当然对结果非常紧张，因为我总是第一手看到怀孕可能出错的种种方式。而且没有任何保证说明最后每个人都能带着孩子回家。所以那只是又多了一层担忧。

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:30.740
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.820
<v Clara Hochhauser>Yeah.
<v Clara Hochhauser>是的。

00:09:32.820 --> 00:10:02.030
<v Susan Burton>Clara would go home with her babies. That's not where this story is going. She would also be delivering at UIC herself. On a medical drama, when the healthcare provider becomes the patient, it's a twist. But this isn't a twist. It's our premise. We don't know how this matters yet, but it will. It matters that this patient is not a visitor, that she's not someone who leaves, but someone who stays.
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉最后带着她的孩子回家了。这不是这个故事的方向。她自己也会在UIC分娩。在一部医疗剧中，当医护人员变成病人时，这是一个转折。但这并不是一个转折。这是我们的前提。我们还不知道这为何重要，但它会很重要。重要的是，这位病人不是访客，她不是那种会离开的人，而是会留下来的人。

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:08.710
<v Susan Burton>A twin pregnancy at age 40-- there was definitely a chance that Clara would need a C-section, but she wanted to avoid it.
<v Susan Burton>40岁的双胞胎妊娠，克拉拉确实有需要剖腹产的可能，但她想避免。

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:21.390
<v Clara Hochhauser>I wanted so badly to have a vaginal delivery. I did not want to have surgery. But because I was older, because I'm a bit fat, I just was like, oh god.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我非常渴望顺产。我不想做手术。但因为我年龄大了，因为我有点胖，我当时就想，天哪。

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:38.510
<v Susan Burton>Clara also wanted to avoid delivering in the operating room. Typically, if you're having twins, you can labor in a labor room, but you have to deliver in the operating room, even if you're delivering vaginally. It's a safety thing, just be in the right room if something goes wrong. But Clara really did not want this.
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉还想避免在手术室分娩。通常，如果你怀的是双胞胎，你可以在待产室待产，但你必须在手术室分娩，即使是顺产。这是一种安全措施，为了以防万一。但克拉拉真的不想这样。

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:46.040
<v Clara Hochhauser>But the idea of having to deliver in the operating room was horrifying to me. I was just terrified of the OR, more or less.
<v Clara Hochhauser>但必须在手术室分娩的想法让我感到恐惧。我基本上对手术室感到害怕。

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.600
<v Susan Burton>How come?
<v Susan Burton>为什么？

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:57.630
<v Clara Hochhauser>Um, delivering in the operating room always just seems so awful to me. I don't know. It's this really narrow, metal table.
<v Clara Hochhauser>嗯，在手术室分娩对我来说似乎太可怕了。我不知道。那是一张非常窄的金属桌子。

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.440
<v Susan Burton>Uh-huh.
<v Susan Burton>嗯哼。

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:18.280
<v Clara Hochhauser>You're flat on your back. You're not restrained, but where are you going to go? You've got an epidural. You're gonna be hooked up to more monitors. You know? Like, it takes away any of that just safety and autonomy, you know?
<v Clara Hochhauser>你平躺着。你没有被约束，但你又能去哪儿呢？你打了硬膜外麻醉。你会连接更多的监护仪。你知道吗？它剥夺了那种安全感和自主权，你明白吗？

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.160
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.850
<v Ira Glass>One of the things Clara did want was for Mindy, her coworker, to take pictures of the birth.
<v Ira Glass>克拉拉想要的一件事就是让她的同事明迪为分娩拍照。

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:36.800
<v Mindy Figueroa>So I have a side hustle, like a photography-- it's a hobby. But I take our coworker's birth pictures.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以我有一份副业，摄影——这只是个爱好。但我拍我们同事的分娩照片。

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:48.190
<v Susan Burton>Mindy's always taken pictures. Back in college with the little cameras you'd hang on your wrist. Eventually she got a camera with a big lens that attached and taught herself how to use it from YouTube.
<v Susan Burton>明迪一直很喜欢拍照。在大学里带着挂在手腕上的小相机。最终她买了一台带有大镜头并能连接的相机，并从YouTube上自学了如何使用它。

00:11:48.190 --> 00:12:03.130
<v Mindy Figueroa>And then one of my nursing friends was pregnant. And she's like, will you take some pictures of me pregnant? She's like, I don't like to go to places. And so I took a few of her and made them black and white. And they were exceptional. And I was like, huh.
<v Mindy Figueroa>然后我的一位护士朋友怀孕了。她说，你能给我拍些孕妇照吗？她说，我不喜欢去那些地方。于是我拍了几张她的，做成黑白的。照片非常出色。我就想，嗯。

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:19.850
<v Mindy Figueroa>And then when she had her baby, she's like, can you take pictures of my baby? And she paid me. And I was like, you don't have to pay me. But she paid me like $200. And I was like, [GASPS], that's a lot of money. Like, no, I'm not a professional. And it just took off from there.
<v Mindy Figueroa>后来她生完孩子，她说，你能给我的宝宝拍照吗？她付了我钱。我说，你不用付我钱。但她还是付了我大约200美元。我当时想，[喘息]，这可是很多钱。就像，不，我不是专业人士。然后它就这样开始了。

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:25.370
<v Susan Burton>Yeah. Do you take, like if somebody's having a cesarean, would you take a picture of the surgical field?
