<v Mindy Figueroa>Yes. Yeah. I'll take a picture of the bloody canister. And I do ask. I do ask how graphic I can get. Because I will literally take a picture of turd coming out, you know?
<v Mindy Figueroa>是的。是的。我会拍那个沾满血的罐子。我确实会问。我会问我可以拍到什么程度。因为我真的会拍那种屎出来的照片，你知道吗？

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:38.450
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:12:38.450 --> 00:13:06.260
<v Mindy Figueroa>I will photograph it all because I am not squeamish. But there's people who are like, that's too much. Or if they're doing skin to skin and it's a beautiful photo, I make sure I cover the nipple so that they can use that photo. Yeah, I just gauge who they are. How graphic are we doing? And I think Clara was one of those who's like, let's do it all.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我会把一切都拍下来，因为我不晕血。但有些人会觉得，那太过了。或者如果她们在做皮肤接触，那是一张漂亮的照片，我会确保遮住乳头，这样她们就可以用那张照片。是的，我只是评估她们是谁。我们要拍到什么程度？我觉得克拉拉就是那种说，全都拍下来吧的人。

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:21.580
<v Susan Burton>On April 19, 2022, Clara arrived at the hospital for a scheduled induction, which is when you get drugs to start your labor. Now, we flashback to see her on that day. She and her husband, Clay, are crossing the street from the staff garage.
<v Susan Burton>2022年4月19日，克拉拉到达医院进行预定的引产，就是当你注射药物开始分娩时。现在，我们回到那一天。她和她的丈夫克莱正从员工停车场过马路。

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:41.310
<v Susan Burton>Clara's purposeful, leading the way. Clay's just passed the bar, is going into criminal defense, did an internship advocating for victims of police brutality. It matters that you know something about Clay so that he's a little more than just the cut-out husband, more than just the husband watching the main event.
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉目标明确，走在前面。克莱刚刚通过律师资格考试，准备从事刑事辩护工作，做过为警察暴行受害者辩护的实习。了解克莱的一些情况很重要，这样他不仅仅是一个背景丈夫，不仅仅是一个观看主要事件的丈夫。

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:44.510
<v Susan Burton>At the employee entrance, Clara badges in.
<v Susan Burton>在员工入口处，克拉拉刷卡进入。

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:59.750
<v Clara Hochhauser>I was coming in. I was scheduled for an evening induction. And so I thought, yeah, I like that. I like those evening inductions. So I'll come in at night after dinner. And I'll get the initial stuff and then go from there.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我进来了。我预定了晚上的引产。所以我心想，耶，我喜欢那个。我喜欢那些晚上的引产。所以我会在晚饭后晚上过来。我会做初步的准备，然后从那里开始。

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:27.230
<v Susan Burton>On the fourth floor, Clara enters her labor room, Room 5, the best one. That's where they try to put you if you're a staff member. Her co-workers have decorated it for her. She changes into a gown, climbs into bed. Clay sets a Bluetooth Speaker on the windowsill, opens his phone, starts the playlist. "Push It," it's on everyone's playlist. The Beach Boys' "Don't Worry Baby."
<v Susan Burton>在四楼，克拉拉进入了她的待产室，5号房，最好的房间。如果你是员工，他们会尽量安排你住那里。她的同事们为她装饰了房间。她换上病号服，爬上床。克莱把蓝牙音箱放在窗台上，打开手机，开始播放列表。《Push It》，每个人的播放列表里都有。海滩男孩的《Don't Worry Baby》。

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:42.290
<v Susan Burton>And so far, Clara's not worried. Even when she receives the drugs to start her labor, everything feels surprisingly OK. By around lunchtime the next day, she's in some pain from her contractions and is debating whether it's time to go for an epidural.
<v Susan Burton>到目前为止，克拉拉并不担心。即使在她接受了引产药物后，一切感觉都出奇地好。第二天午饭左右，她因为宫缩感到了一些疼痛，并正在纠结是否该打硬膜外麻醉了。

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:54.010
<v Clara Hochhauser>And I felt really, really neurotic about it. Not wanting to get it too soon because I wanted to be able to stay upright and keep moving. But you don't want to wait too long because then you're dying in pain.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我对此感到非常神经质。不想太早打，因为我想保持直立并继续走动。但你也不想等太久，那样你会在疼痛中死去。

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:54.810
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:14:54.810 --> 00:15:13.330
<v Clara Hochhauser>One of my coworkers, a midwife, came in and talked with me and was just like, all right, Clara, it's fine. Just get it. If you're ready, you can get it. It's nothing to get perfect here. If you want it and you know you want it, just do it. So Heather did my epidural.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我的一位助产士同事进来看我，跟我说，好了，克拉拉，没关系。就打吧。如果你准备好了，你就可以打。这里没什么好追求完美的。如果你想要，并且你知道你想要，就做吧。所以希瑟帮我打了硬膜外麻醉。

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:28.770
<v Susan Burton>Heather Nixon, the head of obstetric anesthesia at UIC, charismatic, assertive, boss lady, but not bossy. Let's actually meet Heather on the way into the room. Let's watch her leaving her office downstairs on the anesthesia floor.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟·尼克松，UIC产科麻醉主管，有魅力，果断，老板派头，但不霸道。让我们在去房间的路上见见希瑟。让我们看看她离开楼下麻醉科办公室。

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:49.540
<v Susan Burton>Heather's office-- mini fridge, on top of it a framed photo of four women dressed up for girls' night, a black leather couch with a hot pink pillow where lots of times Heather spends the night. Heather is 51, with two daughters in college. But she's the one with the energy of a college kid-- can sleep anywhere, pop up bright-eyed at weird hours.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟的办公室——小冰箱，上面放着一张四个女孩晚上聚会的合影，一张黑色皮沙发，上面有一个热粉色的枕头，希瑟经常在那里过夜。希瑟51岁，两个女儿都在上大学。但她有着大学生的活力——随处能睡，在奇怪的时间也能精神奕奕地出现。

00:15:49.540 --> 00:16:09.500
<v Heather Nixon>My residents actually did a skit once with me. They do the end of the year roast of the attendings. And it was a skit where it was like 3:00 in the morning and they kept answering the phone. And I was like, hey, it's Nixon from anesthesia. And they're like, we have no idea how you're always awake. You're always like, let's talk about stuff at like 3:00 in the morning. I'm like, I don't know.
<v Heather Nixon>我的住院医生其实有一次跟我演过一个小品。他们会在年底烤这些主治医生。那是一个小品，就像凌晨3点，他们不停地接电话。我说，嘿，我是麻醉科的尼克松。他们说，我们真不知道你怎么总是醒着。你总是说，嘿，凌晨3点咱们来讨论些事情。我说，我不知道。

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:25.260
<v Susan Burton>Heather has lived in Chicago for 30 years, came to the city straight after finishing college. She knew she wanted to be a doctor, but she had to pay off student loans first. So she worked as a shot girl in a bar off Rush Street, T-shirt tied up, abdomen showing.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟在芝加哥住了30年，大学一毕业就来到了这座城市。她知道她想成为一名医生，但她必须先还清学生贷款。所以她在拉什街附近的一家酒吧当过射击酒侍，T恤系着，露出腹部。

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:53.350
<v Susan Burton>Anesthesia itself came as a surprise to Heather. It wasn't a specialty she'd ever thought she'd want to do. But she had a mentor who made her fall in love with it. This is the case for a lot of anesthesiologists. There's a common misconception that this is the doctor who just puts you to sleep. But there's a lot more to it than that. The surgeon has their head down in the surgical field. The anesthesiologist is watching over all of you.
<v Susan Burton>麻醉本身对希瑟来说是个意外。这不是她曾想过的专科。但她有一位导师让她爱上了它。很多麻醉师都是这种情况。有一个常见的误解，认为这只是那个让你睡觉的医生。但这不仅仅如此。外科医生低着头在手术区域忙碌。麻醉师在照看你的全身。

00:16:53.350 --> 00:17:02.510
<v Susan Burton>When Heather discovered the subspecialty of obstetric anesthesia, she had a feeling like, OB, this is my tribe. These are my people.
<v Susan Burton>当希瑟发现产科麻醉这个亚专科时，她有一种感觉，产科，这是我的部落。这就是我的人。

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:20.390
<v Heather Nixon>I think you'll find two types of anesthesiologists-- the ones who are the introverts who like to hide behind the drapes with asleep patients and then there's the type that really like to engage with their patients. And those are maybe the tribe of OB anesthesiologists, the ones who want to be there talking to their patients.
<v Heather Nixon>我觉得你会发现两种类型的麻醉师——那些喜欢躲在帘子后面照顾熟睡病人的内向者，还有那种真正喜欢与病人交流的类型。而那些可能就是产科麻醉师的部落，那些想要在那里与病人交谈的人。

00:17:20.390 --> 00:17:43.200
<v Susan Burton>Many OB anesthesiologists proudly identify the same way. They like awake patients, not sleeping patients. Now we catch back up to the action. And we see Heather enter Clara's room and begin arranging her equipment on a tray. Clara sits erect on the bed, waiting, bed pumped up high, her feet dangling.
<v Susan Burton>许多产科麻醉师自豪地以同样的方式认同。他们喜欢清醒的病人，而不是熟睡的病人。现在我们回到剧情。我们看到希瑟走进克拉拉的房间，开始在托盘上整理她的设备。克拉拉直立在床上等待，床泵得很高，脚垂着。

00:17:43.200 --> 00:18:03.840
<v Susan Burton>She'd asked for Heather to do her epidural like, hey, Nixon you gonna be around? Heather was happy to do it. She does this for all her providers delivering if she can. Not that a resident couldn't do it, but you want to take ownership if, god forbid, something goes wrong. Close on Heather's gloved fingers touching Clara's spine.
<v Susan Burton>她要求希瑟帮她打硬膜外麻醉，就像，嘿，尼克松你在吗？希瑟很乐意这样做。如果可以的话，她会对所有分娩的医护人员这样做。不是说住院医生不能做，但你想要承担责任，万一出事了怎么办。特写希瑟戴着手套的手指触碰克拉拉的脊椎。

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:18.840
<v Susan Burton>Now we're going to watch Heather actually do this procedure. We're going to be talking a lot about procedures on this show, and viewers need to be grounded in them. And does anyone really know what an epidural is, besides the thing you can get for pain when you're having a baby?
<v Susan Burton>现在我们要看希瑟实际执行这个程序。我们要在这个节目中谈论很多程序，观众需要了解它们。除了生孩子时可以用来止痛的东西之外，还有谁真正知道什么是硬膜外麻醉吗？

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:39.930
<v Susan Burton>Now we see Clara take an anxious peek over her shoulder and Heather telling her, no, no, I'll talk you through it. You'd think nurses and OBs, they'd be the most relaxed about the epidural, but they're just as nervous as anyone. From her tray, Heather picks up something that looks like a swizzle stick, like what you use to stir a cocktail.
<v Susan Burton>现在我们看到克拉拉焦虑地回头看了一眼，希瑟告诉她，不不，我会引导你完成它的。你以为护士和产科医生会对硬膜外麻醉最放松，但她们和任何人一样紧张。希瑟从她的托盘上拿起了一个看起来像搅拌棒的东西，就像你用来搅拌鸡尾酒的那种。

00:18:39.930 --> 00:19:02.330
<v Susan Burton>This is actually a large, hollow needle, and its placement in Clara's back, this might cause someone to turn from the screen for just a second. So I'll tell you what Heather's doing. She's inching that needle into Clara's back a little deeper, a little more, until the tip enters what's called the epidural space. Heather finds that space by feel. There's a ligament she's looking for.
<v Susan Burton>这实际上是一根粗大的空心针，它在克拉拉背部的放置，可能会让观众转头一会儿。所以我告诉你希瑟在做什么。她正一点一点地把那根针推入克拉拉的背部，直到针尖进入所谓的硬膜外腔。希瑟通过感觉找到那个空间。她正在寻找一根韧带。

00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:21.730
<v Heather Nixon>And not everyone's ligament feels the same. Some of them feel really rubbery. Some of them feel kind of like crackly or poppily, you know, popping. Some of them feel kind of really, like, gentle, like almost when you pass through, you're like, oh, something's different, but I'm just going to stop there.
<v Heather Nixon>并不是每个人的韧带感觉都一样。有些感觉真的很橡胶感。有些感觉像咔哒声或波普声，你知道，那种破裂声。有些感觉非常温柔，当你穿过它时，你会觉得，噢，有些不同，但我就在这儿停下。

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:47.820
<v Susan Burton>Now, Heather threads a thin plastic tube, a catheter, you know this word, through the large, hollow needle until the plastic tube is inside the epidural space as well. We could go one level deeper with the science, explain that the epidural space is not really a space at all, just potential space. But I won't get into this. Just nod to it so that doctors watching can be like, on this show, they know what they're talking about.
<v Susan Burton>现在，希瑟通过粗大的空心针穿过一根细细的塑料管，导管，你知道这个词，直到塑料管也进入了硬膜外腔。我们本可以再深入一层科学层面，解释硬膜外腔并不是真正的空间，只是潜在空间。但我不会深入这一点。只是点到为止，这样观看的医生会觉得，在这个节目中，他们知道自己在说什么。

00:19:47.820 --> 00:20:02.700
<v Susan Burton>Now, Heather slides the needle off the plastic tube, which stays in Clara's back. Anesthetic drugs will be delivered through the tube, bathing the nerves near her spinal cord. Clara is relieved when the procedure is over.
<v Susan Burton>现在，希瑟把针从塑料管上滑走，塑料管留在克拉拉的背部。麻醉药物将通过管子输送，浸润脊髓附近的神经。当程序结束时，克拉拉感到如释重负。

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:27.580
<v Clara Hochhauser>Even though it wasn't like a painful needle or something like that, it was such a strange, unnerving feeling, that pressure in your spine or whatever. But I got it and it was fine. But then around dinner time, I think, was the first time that trouble started with my epidural, where I didn't really even understand what was happening because it was just in my vagina that I felt pain.
<v Clara Hochhauser>尽管它不像那种痛苦的针头或什么的，但脊柱里那种压力或其他什么的真是太奇怪、太令人不安的感觉了。但我打了，它很好。但后来在晚餐时间，我想，是第一次出问题，我的硬膜外麻醉出现了问题，我甚至都不明白发生了什么，因为我感到疼痛的地方就在我的阴道里。

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:44.790
<v Clara Hochhauser>So I wasn't really-- I wasn't aware of contractions. It was just all of a sudden, it felt like my vagina was just being shredded. I don't know. It literally felt like somebody had a steel toe boot and was just kicking around in there.
<v Clara Hochhauser>所以我并没有——我没有意识到宫缩。这只是突然间，感觉就像我的阴道正在被撕碎。我不知道。这真的感觉就像有人穿着钢头靴在里面乱踢。

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:46.750
<v Susan Burton>Oh, geez.
<v Susan Burton>噢，天哪。

00:20:46.750 --> 00:21:03.150
<v Ira Glass>Clara's epidural is topped up with more drugs. And that does the trick. She's more comfortable right away. A couple hours later, at 11 o'clock, Heather signs out to the anesthesiologist on the overnight shift and fills him in on Clara.
<v Ira Glass>克拉拉的硬膜外麻醉又加了更多药。这马上就起作用了。她立刻舒服多了。几个小时后，在11点，希瑟把病人移交给了夜班的麻醉师，并向他介绍了克拉拉的情况。

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:26.390
<v Heather Nixon>When I was signing out, I was like, hey, this is the deal. It's working, blah, blah, blah. And so I didn't anticipate any problems when I left. Otherwise, I would have probably stayed. In hindsight, now, one of the things I'm always like, for my providers that I'm taking care of, I kind of just stay in the hospital. Because I just want to be the one who's there if anything happens.
<v Heather Nixon>当我移交时，我说，嘿，就是这样。它起作用了，巴拉巴拉。所以我离开时没预料到会有任何问题。否则，我可能就会留下来。回想起来，现在，我总是说的一件事是，对于我照顾的同事，我基本上就留在医院。因为我只想成为万一出事时在那儿的人。

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:40.200
<v Susan Burton>We see Heather get in her car, drive away from the hospital.
<v Susan Burton>我们看到希瑟上车，开车离开了医院。

00:21:40.200 --> 00:22:05.000
<v Susan Burton>Clara had been comfortable when Heather left. But several hours later, she's having the same pain again. And other things are going wrong, too. Her labor is stalling. Her blood sugar is climbing. So are the babies' heart rates. Clara knows she's headed for a C-section, but she requests one last half hour laboring on her hands and knees just to see if that will help.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟离开时克拉拉很舒服。但几个小时后，她又有了同样的疼痛。还有其他事情也在出错。她的产程停滞了。她的血糖在升高。宝宝们的心率也是。克拉拉知道她要去剖腹产了，但她请求最后半小时尝试跪着产，看看是否有效。

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:26.890
<v Susan Burton>Now we see the room, dark, except for the lights of the monitors, Clara on all fours in the hospital bed. Close of her forearms, we briefly glimpsed the tattoos. We remember for a moment that Clara has stripped a layer off for us. There's a lot of vulnerability in what she's allowing us to see. The only other person in the room is Clay.
<v Susan Burton>现在我们看到房间，昏暗，除了监护仪的灯光，克拉拉跪在病床上。特写她的前臂，我们短暂瞥见了纹身。我们瞬间记起克拉拉为我们剥下了一层外壳。她允许我们看到的东西里有很多脆弱性。房间里唯一的另一个人是克莱。

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:43.650
<v Susan Burton>That was another thing Clara had requested, just please, everyone clear out and give me one more try. But the try doesn't work, and there's no more time to waste. The nurses unplug Clara from her monitors and push her cumbersome bed down the hall to the OR.
<v Susan Burton>这是克拉拉要求的另一件事，请，每个人都清空，让我再试一次。但尝试没有奏效，没有更多时间可以浪费了。护士们断开了克拉拉监护仪的连接，把她笨重的床沿着走廊推向手术室。

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:49.000
<v Clara Hochhauser>But I was like, OK, it's gonna be fine. It's gonna be fine. My babies are gonna be born.
<v Clara Hochhauser>但我当时想，没事的。没事的。我的宝宝们会出生的。

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:56.370
<v Mindy Figueroa>And yeah, she called me around 3:00 or 4:00 in the morning that they had decided to do a C-section.
<v Mindy Figueroa>是的，她在凌晨3点或4点左右给我打电话，说他们决定剖腹产了。

00:22:56.370 --> 00:23:07.930
<v Susan Burton>Mindy was at home, asleep, but she got right out of bed and grabbed her camera bag. We see her, strap over her shoulder, hurrying down the bungalow's front steps and into her minivan.
<v Susan Burton>明迪在家睡觉，但她立刻起床拿起了她的相机包。我们看到她背着带子，匆忙跑下平房的前台阶，进入她的小货车。

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:27.560
<v Mindy Figueroa>So I raced over there. And it was a little bit of an urgent case, meaning it's not what we call a crash, like where we literally just rip cords out of the wall and run. It was like, all right, we're doing this now. Let's move. So I'm only 10 minutes away from the hospital when there's no traffic.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以我冲向那里。这是一个稍微紧急的病例，意味着它不是我们所说的“冲刺”，就像那种我们直接把线从墙上扯下来跑的那种。这就像，好的，我们现在就做这个。动起来吧。所以当没有交通时，我离医院只有10分钟的路程。

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:47.220
<v Mindy Figueroa>So I raced over there. And I parked on the street because I'm like, it's an urgent case. I need to go because they might start before I get there. I even got a parking ticket that day. Because at night, you don't pay for the meters, but I was there so long that in the morning, they gave me a ticket. But, anyways.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以我冲向那里。我把车停在街上，因为我想，这是一个紧急病例。我需要走，因为他们可能在我到之前就开始了。那天我还收到了停车罚单。因为在晚上，你不用付停车费，但我待得太久了，以至于早上他们给了我一张罚单。但是，不管怎样。

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:57.900
<v Susan Burton>Up on the fourth floor, Mindy takes a plastic-wrapped surgical suit from a closet and pulls the coverall on over her clothes. Then she turns toward Clara's OR.
<v Susan Burton>在四楼，明迪从衣柜里拿出一套塑料包装的手术服，把连体服套在衣服外面。然后她转向克拉拉的手术室。

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:21.420
<v Mindy Figueroa>And I walked in when she was on the operating room table. And I actually have a beautiful photo of her. She has not seen these photos-- or I gave them to her, but I don't think she's ever been able to look at them. But it's the door of the operating room halfway open. And she's laying there with her arms out, and she's looking at me smiling.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我走进手术室时，她还在手术台上。我确实有一张她的美丽照片。她没见过这些照片——或者我给了她，但我觉得她从来没能看它们。但那是手术室的门半开着。她躺在那儿，双臂张开，看着我微笑。

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:49.800
<v Mindy Figueroa>So then, when I got there and I took that picture, just her joy and I almost sensed relief. She knew I was there. And we are all either night shift nurses or day shift nurses. And even though we're all one team, there's more of a friendship and bond. Day shift nurses, we all know how we work. And night shift, we don't work with them ever. So we don't know how they roll.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以，当我到那里拍下那张照片时，仅仅是她的喜悦，我甚至感到了解脱。她知道我在那儿。我们要么是夜班护士，要么是白班护士。尽管我们都是一个团队，但有一种更深的友谊和纽带。白班护士，我们都知道我们是怎么工作的。而夜班，我们从来不和他们一起工作。所以我们不知道他们的风格。

00:24:49.800 --> 00:25:15.600
<v Mindy Figueroa>So I think that smile was like, I'm having my babies. You made it to take my pictures. You're here. You're one of me, one of our team. So yeah, and I, too, was like, yes, I'm here for you. And [SIGHS], this is why this case is so hard because I feel like I failed her 100%.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以我认为那个微笑就像是，我要生孩子了。你赶来拍我的照片了。你在那儿。你是我的同类，我们团队的一员。所以是的，我也觉得，是的，我在这里支持你。而且[叹气]，这就是为什么这个病例如此艰难，因为我觉得我辜负了她100%。

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:26.950
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, the failures in Clara's surgery and where those lead. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来的内容，克拉拉手术中的失败以及那些导致的后果。一分钟后从芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:37.650
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show-- "Chicago Hope." We're hearing a story from the new season of The Retrievals podcast. Susan Burton takes up where she left off.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——“芝加哥的希望”。我们正在听《取回者》播客新一季的故事。苏珊·伯顿从上次结束的地方继续。

00:25:37.650 --> 00:26:00.050
<v Susan Burton>When Mindy arrives, Clara is still being prepped for surgery. She's flat on her back in the OR, which is obviously not where she wanted to be. The L&D operating rooms at UIC are relatively small, out of another era. Mint green tiles on the walls, like you're stepping into the showers at a YWCA. Harsh light.
<v Susan Burton>当明迪到达时，克拉拉仍在为手术做准备。她平躺在手术室里，这显然不是她想待的地方。UIC的劳产科手术室相对较小，像是另一个时代的产物。墙上贴着薄荷绿的瓷砖，就像走进YWCA的淋浴间一样。刺眼的灯光。

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:15.130
<v Susan Burton>But Clara is calm. We see her now through Mindy's viewfinder, her eyes closed, the expression on her face. My god, it's the embodiment of a word whose use is rarely earned-- beatific.
<v Susan Burton>但克拉拉很冷静。我们现在通过明迪的取景器看着她，她闭着眼睛，脸上的表情。天哪，这是一个罕用的词的化身——充满神圣感。

00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:42.900
<v Mindy Figueroa>And I took pictures of everything. I took pictures of Clay and her holding hands. I took pictures of the teams waiting, two different teams for two different babies, pictures of anesthesia working. I like to take pictures of people there because we never get to do that because of privacy laws. So for them to know that I'm on their team, it's almost like, here's a picture of you actually in a case.
<v Mindy Figueroa>我拍下了一切。我拍了克莱和她牵手的照片。我拍了团队等待的照片，两个不同宝宝的两个不同团队，拍了麻醉工作的照片。我喜欢拍在那儿的人的照片，因为因为隐私法我们从没机会做。所以让他们知道我站在他们这边，就像是，这是一张你在手术中的照片。

00:26:42.900 --> 00:27:04.420
<v Susan Burton>Surgery's getting closer now. There are poles on either side of Clara's bed, and soon a giant piece of medical paper will be stretched between the poles, blocking Clara's view of her abdomen. This paper is called the drape, and it both protects the surgical field from contamination and protects the patient from the sight of her open body.
<v Susan Burton>手术越来越近了。克拉拉床的两边都有杆子，很快一大块医疗用纸就会在杆子之间拉开，挡住克拉拉看她腹部的视线。这张纸叫手术巾，它既保护手术区域免受污染，又保护病人免受看自己身体裸露的惊吓。

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:35.390
<v Susan Burton>The anesthesiologist has added more drugs to Clara's epidural, making it strong enough to get her through the surgery. Now the doctors need to test to see if the anesthesia is working. Tight on the skin test, it's so weirdly basic-- the anesthesiologist poking Clara with a broken tongue depressor. Another doctor does something more severe-- clamping Clara's belly hard with a surgical instrument. Clara feels none of it. And that means they're ready to go.
<v Susan Burton>麻醉师在克拉拉的硬膜外麻醉里加了更多的药，强度足以让她度过手术。现在医生需要测试麻醉是否起作用。特写皮肤测试，它真的很基本，太奇怪了——麻醉师用一根折断的压舌板戳克拉拉。另一位医生做了更严重的动作——用手术器械用力夹克拉拉的肚子。克拉拉完全没感觉。这意味着他们准备开始了。

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:53.270
<v Susan Burton>There's a decision to make about the C-section, how much of this procedure to show. Like Mindy said, how graphic are we doing? We're not going for gore on this series, for showing open bodies for the kick of it. But we do want visceral, all the meanings of that word.
<v Susan Burton>关于剖腹产有一个决定要下，展示多少这个程序。就像明迪说的，我们要拍到什么程度？我们在这个系列中不是为了追求血腥，不是为了为了刺激而展示敞开的身体。但我们确实想要内脏的，那个词的所有含义。

00:27:53.270 --> 00:28:14.350
<v Susan Burton>Viscera, literally, that's what's inside a patient's abdomen. But also how that word is applied to art. Art that's visceral-- it's of the body, it attends to the body. Hopefully, it makes you feel something in your body. A visceral aesthetic, muscular, close to the bone. Raw but not sensational, not shying away.
<v Susan Burton>内脏，字面上看，这就是病人腹部里的东西。但也包括这个词如何应用于艺术。内脏的艺术——它是身体的，它关注身体。希望它能让你在身体里感受到什么。一种内脏的美学，肌肉发达，接近骨骼。原始但不耸人听闻，不避讳。

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:42.040
<v Susan Burton>OK, enough self-regard, enough theory. What does this mean in practice? Now the surgeon calls out, incision, and then cuts into Clara's abdomen, just above the pubic bone. We're not seeing the scalpel in her flesh, OK, but just know that this kind of cut is called a bikini incision. Bikini sounds tiny, but this cut is big. Once Clara's abdomen is open, the surgeon needs to reach the uterus.
<v Susan Burton>好了，自恋够了，理论够了。在实践中这意味着什么？现在外科医生叫道，切口，然后切开克拉拉的腹部，就在耻骨上方。我们没看到手术刀切入她的肉里，好的，但只要知道这种切口叫比基尼切口。比基尼听起来很小，但这个切口很大。一旦克拉拉的腹部打开，外科医生需要到达子宫。

00:28:42.040 --> 00:29:09.160
<v Susan Burton>The uterus is behind a pair of muscles. Now we see, actually see, the surgeon and the OB resident position themselves on either side of Clara and pull. These two women in scrubs pull so hard to separate this muscle that their bodies lean back, like in tug of war. This is a very physical surgery. When Clara is a nurse in a C-section, she either covers her eyes or looks away for this part.
<v Susan Burton>子宫在一对肌肉后面。现在我们看到，实际看到，外科医生和产科住院医生站在克拉拉的两侧拉。这两位穿着刷手服的女性拉得如此用力以至于要分开这块肌肉，她们的身体向后倾斜，就像在拔河。这是一个非常物理的手术。当克拉拉作为护士参与剖腹产时，她要么遮住眼睛，要么在那部分移开视线。

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:23.810
<v Susan Burton>But of course, today Clara is the patient. We go behind the drape now, tight on her face, but something's different, radically different. It was just moments ago that she was beatific, but now her mouth is tight.
<v Susan Burton>但当然，今天克拉拉是病人。我们现在走到手术巾后面，紧贴她的脸，但有些东西不同了，彻底的不同了。就在刚才她还充满神圣感，但现在她的嘴唇紧绷。

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:29.130
<v Clara Hochhauser>I just remember, all of a sudden just being like, oh my god, I don't feel good, you know?
<v Clara Hochhauser>我只记得，突然间就觉得，噢天哪，我不舒服，你知道吗？

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:32.070
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:41.970
<v Clara Hochhauser>And I don't know, I assumed I was just panicking. I assumed that I just needed to relax a little bit.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我不知道，我以为我只是在恐慌。我以为我只是需要放松一点。

00:29:41.970 --> 00:30:03.130
<v Susan Burton>A few years earlier, Clara had had a panic attack during another surgery at UIC, an eye surgery, cataracts, weird to have them at such a young age. In the surgery, a different kind of paper drape had been unfolded over Clara's whole body, like a death shroud, so that she was entirely covered in blue paper except for a hole over one eye.
<v Susan Burton>几年前，克拉拉在UIC的另一次手术中经历过一次惊恐发作，眼科手术，白内障，这么年轻得白内障很奇怪。在那次手术中，另一张纸巾盖在克拉拉全身，像裹尸布一样，让她整个人被蓝纸覆盖，除了眼睛上的一个洞。

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:14.740
<v Susan Burton>And then she'd heard the doctor say, you need to give her something else, she's hyperventilating. All through her pregnancy, she'd been worried that if she needed a C-section, she would have a panic attack again.
<v Susan Burton>然后她听到医生说，你需要给她点别的，她过度换气了。整个孕期，她一直担心如果需要剖腹产，她会再次惊恐发作。

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:22.540
<v Clara Hochhauser>And so I had spoken with Dr. Nixon about strategies for how to deal with that if I panic.
<v Clara Hochhauser>所以我曾和尼克松医生讨论过如果我惊恐该怎么办的策略。

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:23.260
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:38.740
<v Clara Hochhauser>And we talked about the different medicines they can give, and that sometimes in that case, nitrous oxide is a really nice, you know, just breath the gas for a little bit. 'Cause they don't last that long. So maybe you can just breathe the gas for a minute or so. And it'll pass, and then you'll be fine, you know?
<v Clara Hochhauser>我们讨论了他们能给的不同药物，有时在这种情况下，一氧化二氮是一个非常好的选择，你知道，就是吸入气体一会儿。因为它们持续时间不长。所以也许你可以吸气一分钟左右。它会过去，然后你就会好的，你知道吗？

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:39.460
<v Susan Burton>Yeah.
<v Susan Burton>是的。

00:30:39.460 --> 00:31:23.790
<v Clara Hochhauser>So I asked, can I just have some gas or something? And he put the mask on. And it just was getting worse. And I remember feeling very nauseous and just saying that I wasn't OK. And then everything was really fuzzy and hazy. And I am not, I guess, thankfully, able to necessarily recall it in as much detail as I could at first. But it was an unfathomable amount of pain. And I remember begging them to stop.
<v Clara Hochhauser>所以我问，我能吸点气或者什么的吗？他戴上了面具。但这反而更糟了。我记得感到非常恶心，只是说我不行了。然后一切都变得非常模糊。我想，幸运的是，我可能无法像开始时那样详细地回忆起来。但这是一种无法想象的痛苦。我记得恳求他们停下。

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:52.270
<v Mindy Figueroa>And it wasn't until she just kept begging like, please, please, please. And one of the attendings, she looked at me and told me to tell her that it was OK. She told me to tell her partner, please tell him it's OK. So at that moment, I was no longer a friend or an advocate. I was more like a staff member being told by the doctor, it's fine.
<v Mindy Figueroa>直到她一直恳求说，求求你，求求你，求求你。其中一位主治医生看着我，告诉我要告诉她一切都会好的。她让我告诉她的伴侣，请告诉他没事的。所以在那一刻，我不再是朋友或拥护者。我更像是一个被医生告知一切正常的工作人员。

00:31:52.270 --> 00:32:12.120
<v Mindy Figueroa>So I remember telling Clay, like, Clay this is normal. This sometimes happens. And Clay, I remember his body language was very like, yeah, OK, I hear you, but don't talk to me. And I remember being like, oh my gosh. Because he was so stressed out.
<v Mindy Figueroa>所以我记得告诉克莱，嘿，克莱这是正常的。这有时会发生。我记得克莱的肢体语言非常像，是的，好吧，我听到了，但别跟我说话。我记得我当时想，噢天哪。因为他压力太大了。

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:38.000
<v Mindy Figueroa>That's one thing I regret the most, that that provider looked at me and said, tell them it's OK, and that I did what she told me to, knowing that it wasn't OK. I think that's one of my biggest things that I replay over and over again, that I didn't say, it's not OK. Can we stop?
<v Mindy Figueroa>这是我最后悔的一件事，那个医护人员看着我说，告诉他们没事的，而我照她说的做了，明明知道事实并非如此。我想这是我一遍又一遍重复的最重要的事情之一，我没说，情况不对。我们能停下吗？

00:32:38.000 --> 00:33:01.720
<v Susan Burton>Clara's anesthesia is not working. She is now in the middle of major abdominal surgery, and she can feel that surgery. Clara knew this could happen. She had seen this happen. Intraoperative pain during cesarean-- healthcare providers know about it. Patients don't.
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉的麻醉没有起作用。她现在正处于大型腹部手术的中间，她能感觉到那个手术。克拉拉知道这可能会发生。她见过这种情况。剖腹产期间术中疼痛——医护人员都知道。病人不知道。

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:23.690
<v Susan Burton>When Clara herself first started working on L&D, and patients in labor would tell her, for my last baby, I had a C-section, and I felt everything, Clara would think to herself, that can't be true, there's no way. Who would do that? And then she saw it happen. And now it is happening to her.
<v Susan Burton>当克拉拉刚开始在劳产科工作时，分娩的病人告诉她，生上一个孩子时，我剖腹产了，我感觉到了所有的一切，克拉拉会对自己想，那不可能，没那种事。谁会那样做？然后她看到了这种情况。现在它发生在自己身上。

00:33:23.690 --> 00:33:52.730
<v Susan Burton>Visceral, that key word, what Clara is feeling is visceral pain, meaning pain that originates inside her abdomen. When the doctors did the skin test, Clara didn't feel it, right? She was numb. But sometimes a cesarean patient is numb when you test her skin. And when you open her up, you find she's not. There are a lot of reasons for this, but in the moment, the reason for the pain doesn't totally matter. What matters is that you treat it.
<v Susan Burton>内脏，那个关键的词，克拉拉感到的是内脏痛，意思是起源于她腹部深处的疼痛。当医生做皮肤测试时，克拉拉没感觉，对吧？她麻木了。但有时剖腹产病人测试皮肤时麻木。当你打开她时，发现并没有。这有很多原因，但在那一刻，疼痛的原因并不重要。重要的是你治疗它。

00:33:52.730 --> 00:34:18.710
<v Susan Burton>The babies, we missed the delivery, but we see them now at the edge of the room in newborn warmers. And we understand they are healthy, that they are OK. But Clara is not. We see Clara punching the drape, as if trying to hit the OBs. What are the OBs doing on the other side of the drape? What are they understanding? What are they feeling? Why are they ordering Mindy to tell Clara's husband everything is all right?
<v Susan Burton>宝宝们，我们错过了分娩，但我们现在在房间边缘的新生儿保温箱里看到了他们。我们明白他们很健康，他们没事。但克拉拉不是。我们看到克拉拉击打手术巾，好像试图打那些产科医生。产科医生在手术巾的另一边在做什么？他们理解什么？他们感觉什么？为什么他们命令明迪告诉克拉拉的丈夫一切正常？

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:37.510
<v Susan Burton>We don't know the answers to these questions yet. We're on the side of the drape with Clara and the anesthesiologist, who's giving her supplemental drugs-- fentanyl, ketamine. Is he doing the right thing, giving her those drugs? We don't know this yet, either. But the feeling we convey now is that something is wrong, very wrong.
<v Susan Burton>我们还不知道这些问题的答案。我们和克拉拉及麻醉师在手术巾的这一边，他正在给她补充药物——芬太尼、氯胺酮。他在做正确的事情吗，给她那些药？我们也不知道。但我们现在传递的感觉是有些不对劲，非常不对劲。

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:53.190
<v Susan Burton>What's going on inside for Clara is that all these drugs have her hallucinating. Is this the kind of show where we do a magical realism thing of inhabiting her consciousness? I mean, it could be, yes, why not use it this way? Why not use it to show what Clara is seeing?
<v Susan Burton>克拉拉内部发生的事情是所有这些药物让她产生了幻觉。这是那种我们会做那种沉浸在意识里的魔幻现实主义的事情吗？我的意思是，可以的，是的，为什么不这样用呢？为什么不用它来展示克拉拉正在看到的东西？

00:34:53.190 --> 00:35:13.180
<v Susan Burton>A story I heard-- I'll say this fast-- a mother is given ketamine during her C-section. Her baby is born and the nurse brings her the baby. And the patient says, this is a lovely baby, but this is not my baby. My baby does not have two heads. I heard this story from a doctor. It happened while she was still a resident.
<v Susan Burton>我听过的一个故事——我会快点说——一个母亲在剖腹产时被注射了氯胺酮。她的宝宝出生了，护士把宝宝拿给她。病人说，这是一个可爱的宝宝，但这不是我的宝宝。我的宝宝没有两个头。我从一个医生那里听到的这个故事。那是在她还是住院医生时发生的。

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:33.400
<v Susan Burton>And she told me that after that woman, she never used ketamine for a cesarean patient again. What Clara is seeing, we see now, colors and shapes. If you're a certain kind of person, you might be fine looking at an open abdomen but freaked out looking at hallucinations. Clara feels unable to communicate.
<v Susan Burton>她告诉我，在那位妇女之后，她再也没给剖腹产病人用过氯胺酮。克拉拉正在看到的东西，我们现在看到了，色彩和形状。如果你是某种类型的人，你看敞开的腹部可能没事，但看幻觉会吓坏。克拉拉觉得无法沟通。

00:35:33.400 --> 00:36:03.680
<v Mindy Figueroa>She was just so slurry, like please, please stop. No, no, no. She sounded like she was out of her body at that point. And I almost started thinking like, I know she's had issues with post-traumatic stress type issues. So I'm like, maybe this is not pain thing. Maybe it's a psychological thing.
<v Mindy Figueroa>她说话非常含糊，就像求求你，求求你停下。不，不，不。她听起来在那一刻灵魂出窍了。我差点开始想，我知道她有创伤后应激障碍的问题。所以我心想，也许这不是疼痛问题。也许是心理问题。

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:25.370
<v Mindy Figueroa>And this happens a lot where patients complain of pain and we brush it off as, she's anxious. It's just pressure. But it wasn't. Because she would feel anytime they touched or did the cautery. Like, she would say the burning, she could feel the burning.
<v Mindy Figueroa>这种情况经常发生，病人们抱怨疼痛，我们却以她很焦虑。只是压力。打发掉。但并不是。因为她能感觉到他们触摸或使用电灼时的任何感觉。就像，她说烧灼，她能感觉到烧灼。

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:45.930
<v Susan Burton>The cautery instrument is called the Bovie. It stanches bleeding. And the red hot pain of it is something Clara remembers precisely. Something that years later, would wake her up in the middle of the night. The sear of that instrument, Clara, exclaiming, ow, and a doctor saying, we'll move to the other side. What?
<v Susan Burton>电灼器械叫博维（Bovie）。它止血。那种红热的疼痛是克拉拉准确记得的。多年后，会在深夜把她惊醒的事情。那种器械的灼烧，克拉拉惊呼，哎哟，医生说，我们移到另一边。什么？

00:36:45.930 --> 00:37:06.820
<v Susan Burton>There's still a lot of surgery left after the delivery. And now we're going to see a part I know some people might not want to see. Brace for it. I'm giving you the warning. The doctor is lifting Clara's uterus out of her body and resting it on her abdomen. It's like a giant, shiny Easter egg. The uterus has been exteriorized. That's what this is called.
<v Susan Burton>分娩后手术还有很多。现在我们要看一部分我知道有些人可能不想看的内容。做好准备。我给你预警。医生正把克拉拉的子宫从她体内拿出来，放在她的腹部上。它就像一个巨大的、闪亮的复活节彩蛋。子宫被外翻了。这就是它的名称。

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:32.940
<v Susan Burton>And the reason we're seeing this is-- please remember that Clara can feel this, can feel her organ being lifted out of her body. Oh, and at some point during all of this, Clara starts throwing up. Now, the surgeon sticks a good portion of her forearm into Clara's body. What we see in the shot is just the arm disappearing into the abdomen. She's using a pad of gauze to check for internal bleeding.
<v Susan Burton>我们看到这个的原因是——请记住克拉拉能感觉到这个，能感觉到她的器官被提到了体外。噢，而且在这一切的某个时候，克拉拉开始呕吐。现在，外科医生把她前臂的大部分伸进克拉拉的体内。我们在镜头里看到的就是那条手臂消失在腹部里。她正用一块纱布检查是否有内出血。

00:37:32.940 --> 00:38:01.060
<v Susan Burton>Again, the point is, Clara is feeling this. Now we hear her scream, "Why are you doing this? Make it stop." And now we do, as if on her command, we leave the surgery. We need to get out of this OR where bad things are happening and we don't understand why. Outside of the operating room, an obstetric anesthesiologist named Cory is arriving for his 7:00 AM shift.
<v Susan Burton>再说一次，重点是，克拉拉正在感觉到这个。现在我们听到她尖叫，你们为什么要这样做？让它停下。现在我们真的像在她的命令下，离开了手术。我们需要离开这个正在发生坏事且我们不明白原因的手术室。手术室外，一位叫科里的产科麻醉师正赶来他早上7点的班。

