<v Clara Hochhauser>And I remember seeing Cory, and he came right by down my face. 'Cause I was still on the table, but all the drapes, everything was gone and done. And Cory was right there. And I remember, I was just like, oh, Cory, you'll help me. I was just like, oh, you'll help me.
<v Clara Hochhauser>我记得看到科里，他来到我的脸边。因为我还在台上，但所有的手术巾，一切都拆掉结束了。科里就在那儿。我记得，我当时就像，噢，科里，你会帮我的。我当时就像，噢，你会帮我的。

00:38:52.790 --> 00:39:21.200
<v Susan Burton>Cory, Clara knows him, trusts him, unlike the other anesthesiologist. She had no idea who that guy was. We see some relief in Clara's face and we see Cory holding back tears. And even though there's no resolution or total explanation, at least we know that Clara is being cared for. Cory wheels Clara to the recovery room, the PACU, and as soon as she's settled, Cory goes down the hall and calls his boss-- Heather.
<v Susan Burton>科里，克拉拉认识他，信任他，不像那个别的麻醉师。她根本不知道那个家伙是谁。我们在克拉拉的脸上看到了一些解脱，我们看到科里忍着泪水。尽管没有解决办法或完全的解释，至少我们知道克拉拉得到了照顾。科里把克拉拉推到恢复室，PACU，她一安顿下来，科里就沿着走廊给他的老板打电话——希瑟。

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:28.240
<v Heather Nixon>He calls me and he's like, Heather, something happened. And I was like, OK. What's going on?
<v Heather Nixon>他打给我说，希瑟，出事了。我说，好的。怎么了？

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:35.640
<v Susan Burton>Heather was at home. She'd been at the hospital so long the night before that she got the day off. Cory told her about Clara.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟在家。她前一晚在医院待了太久，所以那天休息。科里告诉了她克拉拉的事。

00:39:35.640 --> 00:40:15.170
<v Heather Nixon>Oh my god, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no. That just can't be. And I was like, Cory, what happened? He's like, I don't even know. And I just felt-- I felt horrible. I felt like I shouldn't have left the hospital, that I should have just stayed until she was safely taken care, that she had delivered, that I had let her down in that. And then I got mad. And then I got really mad.
<v Heather Nixon>噢天哪，不不不不不，这不可能。我说，科里，发生了什么？他说，我甚至不知道。我只是感到——我感到很可怕。我觉得我不该离开医院，我本该留在那里直到她安全地被照顾好，她已经分娩了，我在这点上辜负了她。然后我生气了。然后我真的非常生气。

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:37.010
<v Susan Burton>Heather is mad because she's in charge here. And she's an anesthesiologist. The thing the anesthesiologist is in the room to do is manage pain. Cutting someone's body open and then operating when they can feel it, that is not supposed to happen. That's something from history or from war.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟很生气，因为她是这里的负责人。她是麻醉师。麻醉师在房间里要做的就是管理疼痛。把人的身体打开，然后在她能感觉到的时候手术，这不应该发生。那是历史或战争中的事。

00:40:37.010 --> 00:41:04.300
<v Susan Burton>But in the United States, it happens 100,000 times a year. That's the best estimate of how many patients have significant pain during cesarean. Not all of these patients feel the exact same intensity of pain, or at the same parts of the surgery, or for the same length of time, but the pain they feel is significant. Most people don't know about this. Until recently, I didn't.
<v Susan Burton>但在美国，它每年发生10万次。这是关于有多少病人在剖腹产期间感到剧烈疼痛的最佳估计。并非所有这些病人都感到相同强度的疼痛，或者在手术的相同部分，或相同的时间长度，但他们感到的疼痛是显着的。大多数人不知道这一点。直到最近，我也不知道。

00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:29.580
<v Susan Burton>I learned of it from listeners to season one of this podcast. It doesn't matter if you haven't heard season one. The details are less important than the common experience it described-- pain a doctor didn't listen to, pain a doctor didn't adequately treat. That experience resonated with many listeners, and hundreds of them, mostly women, began writing to me with their own stories.
<v Susan Burton>我是从这个播客第一季的听众那里得知的。你没听过第一季也没关系。细节不如它描述的共同经历重要——医生不听的疼痛，医生没有充分治疗的疼痛。那种经历引起了许多听众的共鸣，数百人，主要是女性，开始写信给我，分享她们自己的故事。

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:52.540
<v Susan Burton>One afternoon, I opened a note that was unlike any I'd received so far. "I had a C-section," the email began, "where anesthesia was not properly administered. I remember telling them I could feel the cutting, the moving of my organs, the burning, shocking, brutal pain, and being told that wasn't possible and that I would just have to tough it out."
<v Susan Burton>一天下午，我打开了一封和我之前收到的都不一样的信。“我做过剖腹产，”邮件开始说，“当时的麻醉没有正确实施。我记得我告诉他们我能感觉到切割，感觉到器官的移动，感觉到那种灼烧、冲击、残酷的疼痛，却被告知那是不可能的，我只能忍着。”

00:41:52.540 --> 00:42:05.670
<v Susan Burton>To me, this listener's experience seemed so out of range that it might be singular. Then I opened two more of these notes. Soon I understood this was a subject that would come up again and again.
<v Susan Burton>对我来说，这位听众的经历显得范围太窄了，可能是个别情况。然后我打开了另外两封这样的信。很快我明白了这是一个会反复出现的主题。

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:34.870
<v Susan Burton>C-sections are the most frequently performed surgery in the world. In the US, there are 1.2 million of them a year. So 100,000 patients a year feeling pain during cesarean, that's 8%. Some people say the rate is even higher. There's no other surgery where this happens, no other major surgery where it would be acceptable for 8% of patients to feel that surgery.
<v Susan Burton>剖腹产是世界上执行频率最高的手术。在美国，每年有120万例。所以每年有10万名病人感到剖腹产时的疼痛，那是8%。有些人说这个比例甚至更高。没有其他手术会出现这种情况，没有其他大型手术是可以接受8%的病人感到手术疼痛的。

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:55.200
<v Susan Burton>8% is a brand new number from a recently completed study. It didn't replace an old number. It's the first number of its kind. Until now, nobody had ever done a study like this, asking thousands of patients in the US and Canada whether they felt pain during their C-sections.
<v Susan Burton>8%是一个来自最近完成的研究的全新数字。它没有取代旧数字。它是同类中的第一个数字。直到现在，从来没有人做过这样的研究，询问美国和加拿大的数千名病人她们在剖腹产时是否感到疼痛。

00:42:55.200 --> 00:43:17.000
<v Susan Burton>But now the study has been done by doctors who recognize that this is a problem and that putting a number to it is a step toward solving it. And this is what this story is about. Not just that there's a problem with severe pain, but that people are trying to solve it.
<v Susan Burton>但现在这项研究是由认识到这是一个问题并且给它一个数字是迈向解决的一步的医生完成的。这就是这个故事的内容。不仅是有严重疼痛的问题，而且人们正试图解决它。

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:42.240
<v Susan Burton>As soon as I finished season one of The Retrievals, people began asking me, what are the solutions to fixing this problem in medicine of dismissing women's pain? I was surprised the first time I got this question about solutions. Obviously, I should not have been surprised. But I didn't have a bullet point list. How to shift an entire culture, how to listen to women patients-- these are really complicated issues.
<v Susan Burton>我一完成《取回者》第一季，人们就开始问我，解决医学中忽视女性疼痛这个问题有什么办法？我第一次得到这个关于解决方案的问题时很惊讶。显然，我不应该感到惊讶。但我没有要点列表。如何转变整个文化，如何倾听女性病人的心声——这些都是非常复杂的问题。

00:43:42.240 --> 00:44:05.700
<v Susan Burton>And solutions had not been the focus of my reporting. I looked at what went wrong. Exploring what went wrong doesn't automatically translate to solutions on how to make it right. But this question made me curious. Well, what are the solutions? It turned out that a bunch of people interested in pain during cesarean were thinking about the same thing.
<v Susan Burton>解决方案不是我报道的焦点。我着眼于出了什么问题。探索出了什么问题并不能自动转化为如何纠正的解决方案。但这个问题让我很好奇。那么，解决方案是什么？结果发现，一群对剖腹产疼痛感兴趣的人也在思考同样的事情。

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:44.780
<v Susan Burton>So here we go, a case study in solving one of medicine's most persistent problems, listening to women patients and adequately treating their pain, told through the experiences of four women-- Clara, Heather, Mindy, and Susanna, who run up against this problem and take it on, with efforts that touch both individual patients and entire countries. How do they do this? What can they change? And what does it cost them?
<v Susan Burton>所以我们开始吧，一个解决医学最持久问题之一的案例研究，倾听女性病人的心声并充分治疗她们的疼痛，通过四个女性——克拉拉、希瑟、明迪和苏珊娜的经历讲述，她们遇到了这个问题并承担起来，努力触及单个病人甚至整个国家。她们是怎么做的？她们能改变什么？这让她们付出了什么代价？

00:44:44.780 --> 00:44:52.510
<v Susan Burton>Back at the hospital, Clara has been moved from the PACU to a bed. It isn't long before she hears from Heather.
