<v Susan Burton>And now we see Clara in her bed, and we follow her gaze out of the window, out of the hospital, down to the street, where we pause for a second over Mindy's minivan, an orange parking ticket on the windshield. And then we continue across the city, arcing back over the same elevated tracks and wide highways we traversed earlier, only now it's dusk instead of dawn.
<v Susan Burton>现在我们看到克拉拉躺在床上，我们随着她的目光看向窗外，离开医院，看向街道，我们在明迪的小货车上停留了一秒钟，挡风玻璃上一张橙色的停车罚单。然后我们继续穿过城市，绕回我们之前走过的同样的那些高架铁轨和宽阔的高速公路，只是现在是黄昏而不是黎明。

00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:50.490
<v Susan Burton>And when we get to the lake, we turn around so that we can see the sunset over those magnificent buildings, their windows fiery with the day's last light. And we hold that shot as the music swells and the credits roll.
<v Susan Burton>当我们到达湖边时，我们转过身，这样我们就能看到那些宏伟建筑上的日落，它们的窗户在一天最后的余晖中闪烁。随着音乐的响起和演职员表的滚动，我们保持着那个镜头。

00:46:50.490 --> 00:47:16.630
<v Ira Glass>Susan Burton. OK, so that was the opening episode of season two of her podcast, The Retrievals. And I kind of said this at the top of the show. As the story unfolds in later episodes of the podcast, it does this thing that I think almost never happens in a big investigative story. It becomes surprisingly hopeful. Doctors take a look at the problem that Susan is documenting, and then they act the way that you would hope that doctors would act. They take it seriously.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·伯顿。好的，以上就是她播客第二季的第一集。我刚才在节目开头也说了。随着故事在播客的后续剧集中展开，它做了一件我觉得在大型调查报道中几乎从不发生的事情。它变得出奇地充满希望。医生们审视苏珊记录的问题，然后他们采取了你希望医生会采取的行为方式。他们认真对待。

00:47:16.630 --> 00:47:39.650
<v Ira Glass>People step forward to try to change things. One of them is the head of obstetric anesthesia from this first episode, Heather Nixon. I'm going to play you a clip from the second episode so you can hear what I'm talking about. It's interesting. By sheer coincidence, Heather Nixon had been scheduled to give a speech on pain during cesareans at a major medical conference before she saw how the anesthesia in Clara's C-section went wrong.
<v Ira Glass>人们站出来试图改变事情。其中之一就是第一集中产科麻醉的主管，希瑟·尼克松。我将为你播放第二集的一个片段，这样你就能听到我在说什么。很有意思。纯属巧合的是，希瑟·尼克松在看到克拉拉剖腹产时的麻醉情况出错之前，就已经预定要在一次大型医学会议上发表关于剖腹产疼痛的演讲。

00:47:39.650 --> 00:48:03.580
<v Ira Glass>And so she had been planning on doing a speech focusing on technical tips. But now she rethought this. She decided to do something much more participatory than a typical medical conference speech. And when the day of the speech came, she began her talk by telling the assembled doctors the story of Clara's cesarean, how Clara felt, what was happening during her surgery, and really what that did to Clara in the aftermath.
<v Ira Glass>所以她原计划做一次专注于技术技巧的演讲。但现在她重新考虑了。她决定做一些比典型的医学会议演讲更具参与性的事情。当演讲当天到来时，她通过向在场的医生讲述克拉拉剖腹产的故事开始她的谈话，克拉拉的感觉，手术中发生的事情，以及那在后果中真正对克拉拉造成了什么。

00:48:03.580 --> 00:48:21.620
<v Ira Glass>And then, the action turns, the story turns to one of a number of very insidery, almost behind-the-scenes moments where doctors are trying to be honest with each other and with themselves about their own failures and how to fix them. OK, here's a bit of that.
<v Ira Glass>然后，行动转向，故事转向了一系列非常深入的、几乎是幕后的时刻，医生们试图对彼此和对自己诚实，关于他们自己的失败以及如何修复它们。好的，听听这个。

00:48:21.620 --> 00:48:32.660
<v Susan Burton>Usually at a medical conference, people are typing on their laptops or holding their phones up to the screen to take pictures of the slides. This room is still
<v Susan Burton>通常在医学会议上，人们在笔记本电脑上打字，或者拿着手机对着屏幕拍摄幻灯片。这个房间很安静。

00:48:32.660 --> 00:48:50.870
<v Heather Nixon>So this is a harrowing story. It made me mad. It made me sad. It made me frustrated. And it made me really take a deep dive into what is going on in our country. If you go to Google and you search intraoperative pain for cesarean delivery, you will see horror stories across this country.
<v Heather Nixon>所以这是一个令人揪心的故事。它让我生气。它让我难过。它让我沮丧。它让我真正深入研究了我们国家正在发生的事情。如果你去谷歌搜索剖腹产术中疼痛，你会看到全国各地的恐怖故事。

00:48:50.870 --> 00:49:13.070
<v Heather Nixon>And this is really staggering because nowhere else in anesthesia do we allow this to happen. Nowhere, right? If you have a block that doesn't work for an ortho case, your patient goes to sleep. We don't suffer through it. We don't bite the bullet. We don't ask our patient to just take it.
<v Heather Nixon>这真的很令人震惊，因为在麻醉的其他地方，我们不允许这种情况发生。没地方，对吧？如果你有一个脊髓麻醉在骨科手术中不起作用，你的病人就会去睡觉。我们不忍受它。我们不咬牙坚持。我们不要求我们的病人只是忍受它。

00:49:13.070 --> 00:49:22.190
<v Heather Nixon>So I'm going to ask just this room at this moment, how many people in the last month have had a patient who's had severe intraoperative pain?
<v Heather Nixon>所以我现在要问这个房间的每个人，有多少人在上个月有一位经历了严重术中疼痛的病人？

00:49:22.190 --> 00:49:38.310
<v Susan Burton>There are hundreds of people in the room. We watch as half of them raise their hands-- half. Heather had asked how many people in the last month had a patient who had severe pain during their C-section, and half of the doctors raised their hands.
<v Susan Burton>房间里有数百人。我们看着他们中的一半举起了手——一半。希瑟问有多少人在上个月有一位在剖腹产期间感到剧痛的病人，一半的医生举起了手。

00:49:38.310 --> 00:49:49.440
<v Heather Nixon>OK. So this is not rare. We have a group of experts in the room who clearly are experiencing this on a daily basis, and we're just beginning to talk about it.
<v Heather Nixon>好的。所以这并不罕见。我们在房间里有一群专家，显然每天都在经历这种情况，而我们才刚刚开始谈论它。

00:49:49.440 --> 00:50:14.040
<v Susan Burton>And it's crazy that we're just beginning to talk about it because it's a known problem, Heather says. She ticks through all the different kinds of knowing. There's medical literature. There's litigation. There's doctors' own experiences in the operating room. So what that means is that there's an active kind of not knowing, and that act of not knowing is as much of a problem as the pain itself.
<v Susan Burton>而我们才刚刚开始谈论它真的很疯狂，因为它是一个已知的问题，希瑟说。她列举了所有不同种类的知道。有医学文献。有诉讼。有医生自己在手术室的经历。所以这意味着有一种主动的不知道，而那种不知道的行为与疼痛本身一样是一个问题。

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:38.720
<v Heather Nixon>So how do we fix this? How do we think about this? And how do we change what's happening? So why is this happening? All right, I'm going to assume that everyone in this room is passionate about what they do. They love obstetric patients. They're here to learn. They want to do the best. And I'm going to assume that no one gets into medicine to hurt people, OK? We want to help people. So what is the disconnect?
<v Heather Nixon>那么我们如何解决这个问题？我们如何思考它？我们如何改变正在发生的事情？那么为什么会发生这种情况？好的，我假设在这个房间里的每个人都对自己所做的事情充满热情。他们热爱产科病人。他们来这里学习。他们想做到最好。而且我假设没有人进入医学是为了伤害别人，好的？我们想帮助别人。那么脱节在哪里？

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:56.370
<v Susan Burton>Heather's put the questions to the group plainly. How willing they'll be to wrestle with them is anyone's guess. She opens the discussion to the room. The first person to speak is a doctor from New York City named Dan.
<v Susan Burton>希瑟向群体明确提出了问题。他们愿意花多大精力去挣扎，谁也说不准。她向房间开放了讨论。第一个发言的人是一位名叫丹的纽约市医生。

00:50:56.370 --> 00:51:17.690
<v Dan>When we think about why this happens, I think there is this specter in the back of our heads that says, oh crap, the spinal is not working. If I have to induce general anesthesia, there's a chance, because we all have PTSD, that the pregnant airway is horrific, that I'm going to lose the airway and the mom will die.
<v Dan>当我们思考为什么会发生这种情况时，我想在我们的脑后有一个幽灵，说，噢糟糕，脊髓麻醉不起作用。如果我必须进行全身麻醉，有一个机会，因为我们都有创伤后应激障碍，怀孕的气道是可怕的，我会失去气道，妈妈会死。

00:51:17.690 --> 00:51:40.530
<v Dan>And I think that is what is-- I'm gonna say it, that is a fear of mine, that I will lose the airway. And then they'll be like, well, yeah, she was uncomfortable, but maybe you could have muscled through and at least she'd be alive. And so that is the balance that I find when I'm dealing with this in the operating room. And I don't know if other people have had that same discussion in their head.
<v Dan>我想这就是——我会说出来，这是一个我的恐惧，我会失去气道。然后他们会说，好吧，是的，她不舒服，但也许你可以硬撑过去，至少她还活着。所以这就是我在手术室处理这个问题时发现的平衡。我不知道其他人是否在脑海中有过同样的讨论。

00:51:40.530 --> 00:52:01.940
<v Susan Burton>No warm up. No superficial comments before the room really gets going. Dan goes deep straight away. Sometimes I'm scared, he's saying. I'm scared of the biggest thing of all. And in that situation, it seems like the better choice is pain. Heather meets Dan's vulnerability with some of her own.
<v Susan Burton>没有热身。在房间真正开始之前没有肤浅的评论。丹直接深入。有时我很害怕，他说。我害怕最大的事情。在这种情况下，似乎更好的选择是疼痛。希瑟用她自己的一些脆弱满足了丹的脆弱。

00:52:01.940 --> 00:52:24.320
<v Heather Nixon>So I think that that's a wonderful point. We were taught, since fellowship, since I was a baby, I was taught, don't take on the airway. Pregnant is the big bad wolf. Stay away from it. This is how moms die. You'll never recover. The family will never recover. And so, especially towards the end of a case, you might be like, eh, do I really need to?
<v Heather Nixon>所以我认为这是一个很棒的观点。我们被教导过，从研究员开始，从我还是个婴儿开始，我就被教导，不要动气道。怀孕是大坏蛋。远离它。这就是妈妈们死亡的方式。你永远不会恢复。家庭永远不会恢复。所以，特别是在一个病例接近尾声时，你可能会想，呃，我真的需要吗？

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:44.830
<v Heather Nixon>This is the gray area that's ugly and it doesn't feel good. And at the end of the day, I don't feel satisfied with my job because maybe I did the right thing, maybe I didn't. And I'm honest enough to admit it. I've been in that situation several times where I've looked at this and been like, do I really want to do this? Even when the patient maybe had some discomfort.
<v Heather Nixon>这是丑陋的灰色地带，感觉不好。最终，我对我的工作不满意，因为也许我做了正确的事，也许我没有。而且我很诚实，敢于承认它。我好几次处于那种情况，我看着这个，心里想，我真的想做这个吗？即使病人也许有一些不适。

00:52:44.830 --> 00:52:58.510
<v Susan Burton>From the stage, Heather sees one of the women she trained with in fellowship, giving her a look like, same. People start lining up behind the mics. There's a doctor who practices in New Jersey.
<v Susan Burton>在台上，希瑟看到了她在研究员期间培训过的一位女性，给了她一个看起来像，我也是的表情。人们开始在麦克风后面排队。有一位在纽泽西执业的医生。

00:52:58.510 --> 00:53:12.450
<v Nandi>So that for me, it's not just about, oh, the patient's comfortable or she's just exaggerating. It's sometimes that fear that if I do get into a general anesthesia, no one knows how to help me.
<v Nandi>所以对我来说，这不仅是关于，噢，病人很舒服或者她只是夸大其词。有时是担心，如果我确实进行全身麻醉，没人知道怎么帮我。

00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:15.110
<v Susan Burton>And there's Zevy.
<v Susan Burton>还有泽维。

00:53:15.110 --> 00:53:22.590
<v Zevy>I'm Zevy. I'm also from New York City. I actually really appreciate your point about saying the quiet part out loud, where we're here--
<v Zevy>我是泽维。我也来自纽约市。我真的很感激你关于把安静的部分大声说出来的观点，我们在这里——

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:35.510
<v Susan Burton>The part that spinals and epidurals can fail. When do you tell the patient this thing that you know to be true, but that may sound so alarming to them? These things fail. Wait, what? You're telling me this now?
<v Susan Burton>脊髓麻醉和硬膜外麻醉会失败的部分。你什么时候告诉病人这个你知道是真实的事情，但听起来可能对她们非常惊人？这些事情会失败。等一下，什么？你现在才告诉我这个？

00:53:35.510 --> 00:53:48.770
<v Zevy>I was wondering how many people actually say that in the pre-op, discuss that spinals and epidurals actually fail. And if they do fail, we test for it. And we have options to fix it beforehand, even during your consent process.
<v Zevy>我想知道有多少人在术前真正说了，讨论了脊髓麻醉和硬膜外麻醉确实会失败。如果它们确实失败了，我们进行测试。而且我们在术前，甚至在你的知情同意过程中就有选项来修复它。

00:53:48.770 --> 00:54:09.570
<v Andi>I'm Andi. I'm from Stanford. I just wanted to share one of my techniques. Sometimes I either have a patient where maybe there's a language barrier, they're exhausted, they're really scared, and I think my block is working because my block is going to work, but to take my ego out of it--
<v Andi>我是安迪。我来自斯坦福。我只是想分享我的一个技巧。有时我有一个病人，也许有语言障碍，她们筋疲力尽，她们真的很害怕，而且我觉得我的麻醉起作用了，因为我的麻醉会起作用，但要把我的自我从中拿出来——

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:25.250
<v Susan Burton>We are tight on Andi's face so that you can see she's being self-deprecating, not cocky, gently reminding the group to be alert to their own hubris. Doctors continue to come forward, asking questions, offering tips, letting out frustrations.
<v Susan Burton>我们紧贴安迪的脸，这样你就能看到她是自嘲，而不是傲慢，温柔地提醒大家注意自己的自大。医生们继续走上前，提出问题，提供建议，释放挫败感。

00:54:25.250 --> 00:54:31.230
<v Emily>Intraoperatively, I think that the pain versus pressure question is a trope that I really hate in our specialty.
